              {
                "text": "**Analyzing Pixel Placement Patterns**\n\nI'm now diving into the pixel arrangement in the output grids. Observing the surroundings of specific color values, like the number \"2\", is giving me insights. I'm noting the positions of related \"4\"s in the output based on the input locations of \"2\" in several input grids, in an effort to learn the underlying pattern.\n\n\n**Discovering Number Placement Rules**\n\nOkay, I've got some new rules! I see that \"2\"s in the input have \"4\"s as diagonal neighbors in the output grid. Also, \"1\"s in the input have \"7\"s as orthogonal neighbors in the output grid. I'm checking some more training data to see if this pattern holds up, especially with 8 and 6. Train 2 data appears to confirm these rules! Now to look at the other numbers.\n\n\n**Investigating Grid Relationships**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on why the output at coordinate (4,3) is \"4\" instead of \"7.\" It seems the number \"2\" in the input grid influences the placement of \"4\"s in the output, specifically diagonally. I'm cross-referencing this with \"train 1\" and \"train 2\" data to see if these arrangements always appear. Analyzing the color assignments further is also key.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Color Placement**\n\nI'm now grappling with the interplay between different numbers and their resulting colors. I'm focused on why the number '4' seems to take precedence over '7' in specific output cells. I am trying to figure out if it's the priority of \"2\" influencing \"4\" placement over \"1\" influencing \"7\", or if there is a processing order that leads to this outcome.\n\n\n",
